WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.900
Let’s go back in time.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.100
‘We’ve gone from main engine start, we
have main engine start.”

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:17.000
This was the future of cars in 1981, and when
the DeLorean DMC-12 began production with gull-wing

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.240
doors, gullwing as in gulls. Gullwing doors,
doors in unpainted stainless steel panels,

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:28.090
because paint is for cars that could never
convincingly play a time machine, the future

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.439
looked pretty great from every angle.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.600
And then on October 19, 1982, that timeline
ended.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.880
I’ve got a spoiler: this is not a DeLorean.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:43.059
So, like a lot of people, I first heard about
the DeLorean from Back to the Future.

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:46.980
And this fictional version of the car was wildly
popular.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:53.260
But then you find out that the real DeLorean
failed less than two years after production began.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.960
How does that happen?

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.110
Somehow it is still such a popular car that
DeLorean clubs meet around the world just

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.940
to talk about how great it is.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.120
I have a green light.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:13.850
The DeLorean story goes from Hill Valley in
1955, to Margaret Thatcher’s parliament,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:19.060
to war-torn Belfast, Ireland, to a very rainy
parking lot near Washington, DC.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:27.359
That story goes a ton of places, even where
I’m driving right now in this resoundingly

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.429
mediocre vehicle.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.579
When you get into this story, it turns out
that the DeLorean really is a time machine.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.439
“Are you, are you in it right now?”

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:43.890
“I am in it right now, lemme see if I can
flip the camera around.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:44.540
Right?

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:45.480
I can do that, right?

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.639
This entire car is 100% original.

00:01:48.639 --> 00:01:53.739
I like the DMC logo up there, that’s one
thing that’s unique.

00:01:53.739 --> 00:01:58.950
The door ajar light has the gull wing on it.”

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.620
“You have to brag about the gullwing door
in the door ajar light.”

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:14.030
The DeLorean’s many quirks are a collection
of design choices and manufacturing irregularities.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:19.409
Legendary Italian designer Giorgetto Giugiaro
created the design in 1974 and refined it

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:20.519
through 1978.

00:02:20.519 --> 00:02:25.480
Early brainstorms for a mid-engine became
a rear engine, and the stainless panels were

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.600
smoothed out a bit.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.640
Lotus, via legendary racer and engineer Colin
Chapman, provided the car’s

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:32.320
engineering.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.719
The engine was a Renault V6.

00:02:34.719 --> 00:02:39.290
The gullwing doors were in the legacy of cars
like the Bricklin, a failed gullwing indie

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.060
car, as well as gullwings by Mercedes.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:48.740
Those gullwing doors required unusual adaptations,
like a cryogenically twisted torsion bar

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:52.969
and tiny windows - called tollbooth windows,
because they were just large enough to pay

00:02:52.969 --> 00:02:53.620
a toll.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.379
But it actually wasn’t hard to get in and
out of those gullwing doors.

00:02:57.379 --> 00:03:00.739
“Anybody watching this can see it on my
YouTube channel, it’s just Geek Therapy

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:01.739
Radio.

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:07.060
And the myth is that gullwing doors, you can’t
park in any parking lot because if someone

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.489
parks too close to you, you’re trapped.

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:13.299
Doors, as you see, hinge in the roof.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.060
So the doors go up more than they go out.”

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.100
“Rejoice!

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.020
Rejoice!”

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.769
DeLorean fans are always debating about the
car’s quality.

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:29.519
If you say the engine is underpowered, they’ll
say, well it wasn’t that underpowered for

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:30.620
cars of the time.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:35.819
If you say it was heavy, they’ll say, ah
well, look at these Porsches, they were actually

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:38.170
heavier or the same weight.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:39.170
So the arguing?

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:42.440
It’s a rite of passage if you are a DeLorean
owner.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.080
“These guys are all awesome.”

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.160
“Rejoice!”

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.700
“Everytime I see someone post a picture
of a DeLorean on Reddit, you go into the comments,

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:02.230
and it’s usually people posting misinformation,
other people ragging on the car for whatever

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:07.569
reason, and then of course you get like one
or two people, usually - sometimes me - who

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:11.550
then start attacking, going no, here’s the
real history, and it’s like, we’re arguing

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.860
on the internet, who cares?”

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.200
“And it’s Reddit!”

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:15.329
“Right, and it’s Reddit.

00:04:15.329 --> 00:04:19.160
You like the car, you don’t like the car
you don’t like the car, BFD.”

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.780
So, whatever you think of the car, the DeLorean’s
design was improbable.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:25.900
But its creation?

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.840
That, that was almost impossible.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.780
“I remember having a conversation with John
once when we were walking around the plant.

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:43.310
I said to him, you know, what is it about
the car, John, that is sacrosanct to you?

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:48.220
He said, well, there are three things: stainless
steel, gullwing doors, rear engine.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.180
You can do what you like with anything else.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:54.620
My name’s Barrie Wills.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:05:01.660
I was actually the first employee hired out
of the UK car industry into DeLorean.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.340
I’d read about John, of course.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:11.800
He oozed charisma, he was charming, he was
smart,

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:18.660
and above all, he demonstrated a thorough
knowledge of the auto industry.”

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:19.800
We are here.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.040
It’s a liquor store.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.650
Cutty Sark Whiskey — the whiskey with a
boat.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:38.000
I have this whiskey because John Z. DeLorean
and his car were used in this ad to help sell it.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.000
He was that glamorous already.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.340
John DeLorean was a superstar.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.120
He started as an engineer.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.039
This 1958 patent - one of many - was for a
slip drive transmission that he designed.

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:54.380
But he found bigger roles.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:59.670
In 1964, he was considered instrumental in
creating the Pontiac GTO.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:01.199
The car was young, and cool.

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:05.349
It remade Pontiac’s reputation and made
a reputation for John DeLorean.

00:06:05.349 --> 00:06:09.789
And by the 70s, he was in line to be President
of General Motors.

00:06:09.789 --> 00:06:14.080
Being President of GM at that time was like
being next in line to be king.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.280
Here’s a bump.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.350
But DeLorean was chafing at it.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:22.770
He thought it was too corrupt and he thought
they were too square.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:25.800
He was a celebrity and he was a rebel.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:26.800
So he quit.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.960
But he had stayed long enough to make a reputation
for himself.

00:06:35.509 --> 00:06:41.560
This is him in 1974, a year after he quit
GM (critics, of course, said that he was fired).

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:44.340
Long hair, cool shirt, a new look.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:51.669
He said he had surgery due to either an injury
or a jawbone impact correction.

00:06:51.669 --> 00:06:55.970
Critics said was a makeover, the same way
he madeover Pontiac.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:07:01.289
He also married his third wife, the internationally
famous Cristina Ferrare, who he said he’d

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:03.699
literally first seen in the pages of Vogue.

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:06.990
He was the celebrity maverick who fired GM.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.880
And that gave him the mystique to take them
on.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.660
Hope you’re enjoying that authentic turn
signal sound.

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:17.729
I’ve actually composed a song for later
in this video.

00:07:17.729 --> 00:07:21.599
For when we hit our deepest emotional depth.

00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:22.750
It’s a sad song.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:24.300
I’m going to play it later.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:30.400
The new DeLorean Motor Company had glamorous
investors like The Tonight Show’s Johnny

00:07:30.409 --> 00:07:34.520
Carson as well as a unique plan to get investments
from car dealers.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:39.319
“This is a presentation of the DeLorean
Motor Company.

00:07:39.319 --> 00:07:47.939
Our requirements are one: the dealer or his
dealer associates must own $25,000 in DeLorean

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.700
motor company common stock.”

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:56.639
But a brand new factory and car required north
of $100 million in investment.

00:07:56.639 --> 00:07:57.719
That meant government cash.

00:07:57.719 --> 00:08:00.120
There were a few potential timelines.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.920
Early flirtations with a Saudi Arabian investor
and the state of Pennsylvania fizzled.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:10.080
A plan with Puerto Rico got further, offering
a package close to $60 million if DeLorean

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.310
raised $25 million first (and spent it first).

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:20.010
But Northern Ireland, and therefore the UK,
eventually paid up around $120 million, depending

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.630
how you split loans, grants, and guarantees.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:24.520
The location of the factory?

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:25.639
It was difficult.

00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:30.250
“It was 72 acres of...there were cattle
on it.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:38.520
Half a mountain had to be relocated from outside
Belfast to put enough footings onto the site

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.160
to construct the buildings.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.860
Put the steel framework up, apart from the
fact that two streams had to be diverted.”

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:51.320
This was with an insanely fast 18 month time
frame and an even more unstable political

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:52.320
environment.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.740
All this was during “The Troubles,” the
violent sectarian conflict in which Northern Ireland

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:01.340
was fighting over whether they should be part of the Republic of
Ireland or the UK.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.820
And this is why DeLorean got the money.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:09.220
The British government wanted to add stability
and jobs to a violent, unemployment-plagued

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.550
region.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:14.660
Barrie Wills says that it actually didn’t
impact production that much, but riots did

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:18.950
spill into the factory, and they
had to consider stuff like hunger strikes

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.660
in their production timeline.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:27.700
“As each hunger striker died, the Catholic,
Republican workforce, would be called out

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.100
for a day of mourning.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:36.840
The director of personnel was given a program
by the Northern Ireland office of the likely

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:41.000
death days of the hunger strikers so that
we could plan ahead.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:43.080
Like, terrible.”

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:50.580
Any other company would have let off the gas
in this situation, but DeLorean did not have

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:51.320
a choice.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:55.740
They were shipping 1,800 cars in October of
1981.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.420
They had to keep up with their funding obligations.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:01.960
Hire a large workforce, pay bonuses, make royalty
payments.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:03.050
It was a lot.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:05.400
They pulled it all off really quickly.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:08.960
This DeLorean Motor Company infomercial shows
the factory at work.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:14.240
But they needed constant cash to keep this
going - from car sales or the British government.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.240
They needed perfection, but they got reality
instead.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:26.360
Though the company had turned a slight profit
by some measures, there were also almost 5,000

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.390
unsold cars by January of 1982.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:34.080
They were very pricey at $25,000, and they’d
gotten a bad reputation.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:38.770
A 2,000 car recall and performance issues
in the first batch didn’t help.

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:45.380
“The first cars that were shipped out to
the states were not good cars.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.700
We were under immense pressure.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.810
The government had made it clear that we weren’t
going to get anymore money.”

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:56.880
By February, 1982, DMC was in receivership
- basically a forced bankruptcy.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:58.840
By May, production stopped.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:03.940
DeLorean had dipped back for money before,
and the UK, led by conservative Margaret Thatcher,

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:07.070
didn’t want to give more money to an American
company.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:12.370
Rumors of fishy corporate structures, credible
accusations of fraud, and possible millions

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:15.170
in diverted money didn’t help make the sale.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:19.640
So all that had to have made it harder to
raise cash in those last ditch efforts.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.070
Now DeLorean fans, they will tell you that
the company failed because of the recession,

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:29.540
and bad weather, and exchange rates, and they
have a really good point — all that stuff hurt

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:30.630
the company.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:35.400
But it might not have doomed the company had
it not taken on so much risk, again and again.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:37.100
All those bets?

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:40.420
Eventually DeLorean was going to lose one
of them.

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:44.760
And that was all before the flashiest scandal
of all happened.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:56.640
In autumn, DeLorean was arrested for trying
to raise money in an unusual way - agreeing

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.600
to smuggle millions of dollars worth of cocaine.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:00.820
“It was horrific.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:05.280
I was told the night before that we all believed
that John was raising the money.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.480
We didn’t know where it was coming from.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:14.460
I got a call hours before John’s arrest,
to say, bring the workforce together tomorrow, I’m

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:17.540
sorry Barrie, it’s all over, it’s liquidation.”

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.140
DeLorean was eventually acquitted because
of entrapment.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:23.230
He was never convicted of smuggling or fraud.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:24.930
So you have to make a judgment call.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.600
When you see this man, do you see a genius?

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.640
Or an actor, playing a part?

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.340
Maybe he was both.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:39.830
John Z. DeLorean, why did
your car have to die?

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.280
But your dream lives on.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:47.480
Let me show you why.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:54.850
Alright, so if you don’t know what Big Lots
is, it got its store as an overstock store.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:55.850
Bargain bin place.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:13:01.020
And when DeLorean went bankrupt, the parent
company of Big Lots actually bought all the

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:02.020
DeLorean parts.

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:04.950
We’re talking like, the literal stainless
steel panels.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.000
And all those parts went into a warehouse.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:10.750
It looked like the end of the DeLorean story.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:12.040
And then a movie came out.

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:14.820
What is the comment you get over and over
again?

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.140
“This is what makes time travel possible.”

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:20.120
“After the movie, it was all about the flux
capacitor.”

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:22.880
“Flux capacitor.”

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:23.720
“Flux capacitor.”

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:25.800
“Where’s the flux capacitor?

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:29.940
You know after a while you just get worn down
and have to make a flux capacitor.”

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:30.680
“Flux capacitor.”

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:31.620
“Flux capacitor.”

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:32.500
“Flux capacitor.”

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:34.880
“Flux cap-”
“McFly!

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:37.420
McFly or Back to the Future.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:39.260
But usually it’s the McFly.”

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.080
The car in the movie?

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.320
It was a fantasy.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:48.830
It had to go 88 miles to travel in time, and
the original DeLorean speedometer doesn’t

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:49.830
go that high.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.430
They had to modify it for the movie.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:57.510
But little differences like that didn’t
stop the DeLorean from becoming a time machine.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:01.870
After the movie, two communities developed
in the vacuum left by DeLorean Motor Company’s

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:02.870
failure.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:08.010
Today, there’s a strong international roster
of companies able to service DeLoreans and

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:11.920
supply factory original parts, even though
they weren’t a part of the original company.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:16.770
And there are also clubs around the world
where owners can support each other.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:21.190
“Right now I’m the president of the DeLorean
Midatlantic club. We have about 80 members

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:22.890
in the club right now.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:25.030
We do events 4 times a year.”

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:29.870
One of Tiffany’s first events was a DeLorean
MidAtlantic pigrimage to John Z. DeLorean’s

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:30.870
mansion.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:33.760
But club events aren’t just about the past.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.420
They’ve made the car a platform for creativity.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.410
It’s a stainless steel Lego set.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:41.230
“Everybody has their level of modifications
they like.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:45.790
For my own car I’ve actually done custom
reupholstered seats.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:52.000
I’ve upgraded the exhaust to stainless steel
exhaust that’s been from DeLorean Go in Europe.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.100
It sounds great.”

00:14:56.740 --> 00:15:02.780
“So at this point I’ve done fuel lines,
the alternator, fuses and relays, radiator

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:05.980
fans, the entire suspension at this point’s
been upgraded.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:08.470
The car’s been lowered, new tires.”

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:10.800
“We will share parts with each other.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:15.660
Like if someone’s working on something and
we need something, I have extra DeLorean parts.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.440
So it’s kinda neat knowing that you have
part of your car in someone else’s, living

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:21.300
on.”

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:29.780
“I made it and did all the programming and
developed all the software and put it in my car."

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:33.840
This runs everything on the car.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.040
It gets data to the digital dash.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:39.400
“I mean, as most owners will tell you it’s
never over.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.860
Well I of course had to upgrade that.

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:45.160
That’s Lightning McQueen.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.050
He’s in for an overhaul.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:51.500
I doubled the voltage on and that’ll be
ready for the spring.”

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:57.740
There’s one clip in particular I really
like.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:02.320
It’s of the Tri-State DeLorean club and
they’re taking a picture with all their

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:03.200
cars.

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:06.900
Now look at them, look at how they rush to
get out of the way.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:11.830
They are happy to give up the spotlight in
a way that John Z. DeLorean never could.

00:16:11.830 --> 00:16:14.960
This car succeeded because it inspired people.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:18.600
Let’s end this video in the right car.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:19.960
“Perfect.”

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:28.860
“All those people there, the thing that
brings us together is this car, but they’re

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:32.540
all really great people and I consider them
all friends.

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:35.560
It’s a good thing.

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:38.280
I’ve reached the point in my life now where
I really have to choose where I’m going

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:43.190
to spend my time and the type of people I’m
gonna be around.

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:47.900
You’ve got to be really careful where you
spend your time.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:48.940
You don’t want to waste it.”

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.140
So thank you for riding with me today.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:06.370
If you want to check out a Cars and Coffee
like the one I went to in the video, there

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:11.110
might be one in your area, and there’s a
lot of cool cars that people share there.

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:14.569
Thank you also to the Tri-State DeLorean Club
for letting me tag along.

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:17.130
They were all awesome and incredibly nice.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:20.780
Thank you to Barrie Wills for sharing with
me his stories.

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:25.240
They’re detailed further in his book about
his career with DeLorean.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:31.759
Finally, in our membership program the Video
Lab, I am reviewing five or six of the top

00:17:31.759 --> 00:17:36.379
DeLorean books which I read for this piece
and letting you know which you should check

00:17:36.379 --> 00:17:38.950
out and which ones you can go ahead and skip.

